  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1128 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nAs an upper ridge shifts east of the area and deep troughing\nmoves into the western CONUS this weekend, mid to upper level\ndiffluence and shortwave energy will result in scattered showers\nand perhaps some thunderstorms. Coverage and amounts of rainfall\nhave decreased slightly in recent model runs. Some areas may\nremain dry through the entire event, while others see scattered\nlight rainfall but not all day washouts. Friday evening into\nFriday night will see 15-30% rain chances over the northwestern\nCWA. Greatest coverage/chances are Saturday afternoon into\nSaturday night at 30-50%. Deterministic forecast rainfall\namounts are currently 0.00-0.12 inches, with NBM probabilities \nfor >=0.10 inches only at 30-50%.\n\nFriday will continue to be very warm with highs in the upper 70s\nto around 80, but Saturday will see highs in the low to mid 70s.\n\nEarly to middle of next week will see potential for widespread\nrainfall and cooler temperatures closer to normal. This is due\nto ensembles showing a deep mid level system moving into the\ncentral CONUS. However, model spread is still significant with\nthis, which makes sense given the current location of the wave\nis well west of Alaska and has a substantial amount of evolution\nto undergo as it interacts with additional waves before moving\ninto the CONUS. Ensemble clusters are showing little preference\nfor one scenario over another, with differences in timing, \nstrength, and track. Outcomes generally range from a strong \nclosed low moving into the area (worst case:\nWidespread rain, thunderstorms, maybe a severe and flooding\nrisk) to a weaker, positively tiled upper wave (best case: \nScattered showers and storms). Model blend results in 50-70%\nchances for showers and storms Monday into Wednesday. \nConfidence is too low to discuss specifics, but those with \ninterest should continue to monitor.",
